{"hash":"64cb189d993f4086","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":138.209,"said":"And here he is. The tunnel opens, and from the tiers of the Ganesh Mandapam you see him at last: Shri Mahakaleshwar, the Lord who rose from the earth to save his devotees, and stayed. Look at which way he faces. South. Of all the twelve Jyotirlingas, only Mahakal is Dakshinamukhi, facing south, and in Hindu belief the south is the direction of death. He looks straight toward it, for he is the Lord of Time, beyond death itself. If you came back before dawn, at four in the morning, you would find this hall packed for the Bhasma Aarti. The linga is bathed in water and panchamrit, adorned with sandalwood, flowers and bilva leaves, and then covered in sacred ash. Women draw their veils while the ash is offered. Then the lamps rise, and drums, bells and the conch fill the hall. Why ash? Mahakal is the Lord of Time, who dwells in the cremation ground, and ash is what every body becomes; what remains after the fire is Shiva alone. Of the twelve Jyotirlingas, it is Mahakal who is known for this daily offering of ash. Long ago, it is said, the ash came from a funeral pyre; today it is made from cow-dung cakes and the wood of sacred trees such as peepal, palash, shami and bel. To attend, book a place in advance on the temple committee's official website and bring photo ID, and come in the old dress: an unstitched dhoti for men, a saree for women. Fold your hands now. This is your darshan. Mahakal is the third Jyotirlinga of the Stotram. Pilgrims may visit the twelve Jyotirlingas in any order their journey allows; by tradition, the yatra begins at Somnath, the first, and is held complete with darshan at Grishneshwar, the twelfth, near Ellora. For most pilgrims, darshan ends here, at this barricade. But in this virtual yatra you may go further: the silver gate beside the Nandi stands open.\n\nDid you know? Once a year, on Mahashivratri, Mahakal also has a Bhasma Aarti in the afternoon, after the darshan of his floral sehra.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1807},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1823,"end":1942}],"words":[[0.098,0.228,0,3],[0.338,0.252,4,8],[0.603,0.168,9,11],[0.783,0.325,12,14],[1.971,0.12,16,19],[2.103,0.349,20,26],[2.464,0.529,27,32],[3.233,0.192,34,37],[3.438,0.204,38,42],[3.654,0.108,43,46],[3.774,0.337,47,52],[4.123,0.096,53,55],[4.231,0.108,56,59],[4.351,0.409,60,66],[4.772,0.433,67,75],[5.216,0.18,76,79],[5.409,0.24,80,83],[5.661,0.192,84,87],[5.865,0.156,88,90],[6.034,0.445,91,95],[6.683,0.264,97,101],[6.959,0.962,102,115],[8.149,0.096,117,120],[8.257,0.3,121,125],[8.57,0.132,126,129],[8.714,0.312,130,134],[9.038,0.192,135,139],[9.243,0.24,140,143],[9.495,0.216,144,149],[9.724,0.096,150,152],[9.832,0.325,153,157],[10.168,0.156,158,161],[10.337,0.685,162,170],[11.19,0.252,172,175],[11.454,0.457,176,182],[12.788,0.252,184,188],[13.053,0.192,189,191],[13.257,0.216,192,197],[13.486,0.168,198,201],[13.666,0.132,202,204],[13.81,0.637,205,210],[15.361,0.709,212,217],[16.827,0.216,219,221],[17.055,0.24,222,225],[17.308,0.108,226,229],[17.428,0.397,230,236],[17.837,0.865,237,249],[18.942,0.276,251,255],[19.231,0.613,256,263],[19.856,0.18,264,266],[20.048,0.962,267,280],[21.262,0.469,282,288],[21.743,0.469,289,294],[22.404,0.204,296,299],[22.62,0.108,300,302],[22.74,0.349,303,308],[23.101,0.373,309,315],[23.486,0.12,316,319],[23.618,0.457,320,325],[24.087,0.12,326,328],[24.219,0.096,329,332],[24.327,0.529,333,342],[24.868,0.168,343,345],[25.048,0.349,346,351],[26.31,0.12,353,355],[26.442,0.24,356,361],[26.695,0.373,362,370],[27.079,0.337,371,377],[27.428,0.24,378,380],[27.921,0.144,382,385],[28.077,0.204,386,388],[28.293,0.144,389,391],[28.45,0.084,392,395],[28.546,0.312,396,400],[28.87,0.132,401,403],[29.014,0.457,404,408],[29.712,0.349,410,416],[30.072,0.252,417,422],[30.337,0.577,423,429],[31.863,0.204,431,433],[32.079,0.18,434,437],[32.272,0.288,438,442],[32.572,0.252,443,447],[32.837,0.349,448,454],[33.197,0.373,455